**parseAge(text)**

Write `parseAge(text)`: return the age as a number. For anything that isn't a whole number from 0 to 150 (including empty text), throw a `RangeError` with the message `"age must be a whole number from 0 to 150"`.

```js
function parseAge(text) {
  // TODO: validate, and throw new RangeError(...) for bad input
  return Number(text);
}
```

back:
// Why: Throw the most specific built-in error (RangeError for out-of-range values) so
// callers can tell failures apart. Number("") is 0, so empty input needs its own check.
// Docs: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Control_flow_and_error_handling
function parseAge(text) {
  const age = Number(text);
  if (text.trim() === "" || !Number.isInteger(age) || age < 0 || age > 150) {
    throw new RangeError("age must be a whole number from 0 to 150");
  }
  return age;
}

